"The oldest known piece of paper, a map found in a tomb in Gansu, was made in the 2nd century BCE. About 105 CE an official named Cai Lun recorded a way of making paper from bark, rags, hemp, and old fishnets. Paper reached Samarkand by 751 and Baghdad by 793, and by the 1300s there were paper mills in Europe. Printing came next. Buddhist charms were printed from wood blocks in Japan about 764–770, and the Diamond Sutra, printed in China in 868, is the oldest known printed book. About 1041–48 Bi Sheng made movable type from baked clay. In Korea, movable type appeared by the 1200s, and in 1403 the king ordered 100,000 pieces of bronze type. Britannica says this knowledge does not seem to have reached Europe, where a printing press was invented about 1450. The magnetic compass has a shared story. Late in the 11th century Shen Kuo wrote that a needle rubbed with lodestone points south, though slightly east. Britannica says that in the 12th century sailors in China and Europe found, apparently on their own, that lodestone could show them north. Gunpowder was used in China for fireworks and signals by the 10th century; knowledge of it later reached Europe, possibly through the Mongols or the Arab world. In 1402 the Yongle emperor took the throne of the Ming dynasty. He chose Zheng He, a man from a Muslim family in Yunnan who had been captured as a boy and served the Prince of Yan, who became the Yongle emperor, to command great fleets to the “Western Oceans.” Between 1405 and 1433 Zheng He led seven voyages to Southeast Asia, India, the Persian Gulf, Arabia, and the coast of East Africa. The fleets carried silk, porcelain, and other goods, traded at the ports, and brought envoys and tribute back to China, including a giraffe. Education About Asia says the fleet was by and large meant for peaceful diplomacy and trade, though the History of Ming says force was used against those who would not submit, and the fleet did fight: it defeated a pirate leader and captured a king of Ceylon. Calicut and Hormuz were already great trading cities; Malacca, founded only a few years earlier, grew into a major hub in that century (Lesson 9 tells of the older sea roads). After the Yongle emperor died in 1424, the voyages were suspended; one last fleet sailed in 1431. Zheng He died in 1433, and the great fleets never sailed again. Chinese merchants and migrants, however, kept sailing to Southeast Asia, and Chinese communities grew there. About 65 years later, Portuguese ships reached India by sailing around Africa.": "3b7832eae76f",
